# Weather Map — Pressure Isobars Layer  
## Enhancement Spec v1.1

---

## 1. Purpose

This spec introduces a new optional map layer:

**pressure isobars**

Goal:
- add synoptic context to the weather map
- help users understand pressure systems, fronts, and large-scale weather structure
- preserve the existing Map architecture: shared timeline, shared player, weather-owned domain

This is an additive enhancement over Map v1 / v1.1. The existing cloud layer, player model, and timeline model remain unchanged. The current contract already reserves `layers.isobars` as an optional layer slot, so this enhancement formalizes its semantics and implementation.  [oai_citation:0‡Weather Map Data Contract v1.md](sediment://file_00000000993c720abc292795d3e717f1)

---

## 2. Non-breaking Rule

This layer MUST NOT:
- change the master timeline
- change player behavior
- change cloud layer behavior
- introduce frontend-side time alignment logic
- require the map to function only when isobars are available

Map must remain fully operational when:
- `layers.isobars.available = false`

---

## 3. Layer Role

Isobars are a **context layer**, not the primary layer.

They are intended to:
- visualize pressure structure
- complement cloud animation
- improve interpretation of cloud movement and weather systems

They are not intended to:
- replace cloud layer
- act as a standalone forecast product
- provide local observing scoring
- drive map timeline

Default behavior remains:
- `enabled_by_default = false`

This aligns with the current Map overview and data contract, where clouds are the essential animated layer and isobars remain optional.  [oai_citation:1‡Map Overview v1.md](sediment://file_0000000091ac720a9b064d3bcff4655c)  [oai_citation:2‡Weather Map Data Contract v1.md](sediment://file_00000000993c720abc292795d3e717f1)

---

## 4. Temporal Model

Isobars MUST follow the same master timeline as the map.

### Timeline rules
- range: `[-48h … +72h]`
- step: `1 hour`
- frame count: `121`
- current index: `48`

### Alignment rule
Each isobar frame MUST align exactly to:
- `timeline.frames[index].index`
- `timeline.frames[index].t_utc`

Frontend must be able to do:

```text
render isobars.frames[current_index]

without guessing or converting time.

This matches the master player contract and the existing alignment rule across layers.  ￼  ￼

⸻

5. Source Model

Allowed source class

forecast_model

Preferred source type:
	•	hourly pressure field or pressure contours derived from one stable forecast source

Practical source inputs

Acceptable upstream forms:
	•	mean sea level pressure raster/grid
	•	precomputed isobar vector lines
	•	hourly pressure map fields from the same forecast family used by the weather map

Source consistency rule

For MVP-style implementation, use:
	•	one stable source family
	•	one pressure variable definition
	•	one hourly cadence

Do not mix:
	•	multiple forecast providers per frame
	•	multiple pressure semantics inside one run

This follows the same single-path source discipline already defined for cloud forecast sources.  ￼

⸻

6. Data Contract

This layer fills the already-defined layers.isobars block in weather_map_now.json.  ￼

Layer structure

interface WeatherMapLayer {
  enabled_by_default: boolean;
  available: boolean;
  opacity: number | null;
  frames: WeatherMapLayerFrame[];
}

Frame structure

interface WeatherMapLayerFrame {
  index: number;
  t_utc: string;
  available: boolean;
  asset_type: "tile" | "image" | "vector";
  asset_url: string | null;
  meta?: Record<string, string | number | boolean | null>;
}

Recommended asset_type

Preferred for v1.1:
	•	"image"

Allowed later:
	•	"vector"

Why image first

Rasterized transparent overlays are simpler because:
	•	they align easily with cloud layer
	•	they avoid frontend contour rendering complexity
	•	they are consistent with the cloud asset pipeline style

⸻

7. Layer Metadata

Recommended frame-level metadata:

meta: {
  source_name: string;
  pressure_unit: "hPa";
  contour_step_hpa: number;
  min_hpa?: number;
  max_hpa?: number;
}

Recommended layer-level defaults:
	•	enabled_by_default = false
	•	opacity = 0.70

⸻

8. Visual Design

Rendering mode

Preferred v1.1:
	•	transparent raster overlay with contour lines

Visual goals
	•	legible over clouds
	•	readable over base map
	•	not visually dominant
	•	stable across timeline playback

Line style
	•	thin contour lines
	•	subdued but readable color
	•	limited label density

Recommended appearance
	•	line color: light cyan, pale gray-blue, or muted off-white
	•	low to medium opacity
	•	thin stroke
	•	optional sparse pressure labels

Forbidden
	•	thick neon lines
	•	dense label spam
	•	pressure heatmap instead of isobars
	•	strong color palette conflicting with clouds or wind

This complements the current render policy requirement that cloud overlays remain readable and compatible with other layers.  ￼

⸻

9. Contour Policy

Canonical contour step

Recommended default:
	•	5 hPa

Optional extended support:
	•	4 hPa
	•	10 hPa

But one project-wide default should be fixed.

Labeling policy

For v1.1:
	•	labels are optional
	•	if used, keep them sparse
	•	avoid label overlap with dense cloud regions where possible

Smoothing policy

Allowed:
	•	light contour smoothing
	•	simplification for visual clarity

Forbidden:
	•	aggressive distortion of pressure structure
	•	label placement that hides cloud interpretation

⸻

10. Spatial Model

Isobar overlays MUST match the active cloud profile’s spatial domain.

If Map v1.1 uses zoom-aware cloud profiles, then isobars must support the same spatial profile selection logic:
	•	same bbox family
	•	same projection family
	•	same frame index

Rule

Time is controlled by player.
Spatial coverage is controlled by active map profile.

So the isobar layer must be compatible with both:
	•	current timeline index
	•	current zoom-selected spatial profile

⸻

11. Profile Compatibility

If map uses coverage profiles such as:
	•	eu_wide
	•	eu_central
	•	local

then isobars should ideally provide the same profile set.

Recommended structure:

layers.isobars.profiles[]

if the map contract has already been extended to profile-based layers.

If the current implementation still uses one fixed domain, then v1.1 may start with:
	•	one isobar domain only

but long-term target should follow the same zoom-aware model as clouds.

⸻

12. Asset Layout

Recommended path:

assets/weather/map/isobars/

If profile-based:

assets/weather/map/isobars/eu_wide/
assets/weather/map/isobars/eu_central/
assets/weather/map/isobars/local/

Naming

isobars_000.webp
...
isobars_120.webp

or PNG if preferred.

Keep index-based naming for easy player synchronization.

⸻

13. Pipeline Responsibilities

Add an isobar pipeline branch under weather map generation.

High-level flow

fetch pressure field
→ align to hourly timeline
→ normalize to active spatial basis
→ generate contours
→ rasterize transparent overlay
→ publish assets
→ fill layers.isobars.frames[]

Pipeline must handle
	•	hourly alignment
	•	same anchor_utc as clouds
	•	same index range
	•	same profile domain if profile-based map is used

Pipeline must not
	•	change player timeline
	•	ask frontend to generate contours
	•	silently switch pressure providers mid-run

⸻

14. Performance Strategy

Because isobars are optional:
	•	they may be generated after clouds in the pipeline
	•	they may load lazily on frontend
	•	they should not block the core map from rendering

Recommended:
	•	lightweight transparent raster overlays
	•	reuse profile bounding boxes
	•	cache per anchor_utc + profile + frame_index + render_version

⸻

15. Failure Handling

Layer-level failure

If the pressure source is unavailable:

{
  "enabled_by_default": false,
  "available": false,
  "opacity": 0.70,
  "frames": []
}

or aligned unavailable frames if contract requires full arrays.

Frame-level failure

{
  "index": 77,
  "t_utc": "...",
  "available": false,
  "asset_type": "image",
  "asset_url": null
}

Timeline must remain intact.

Map must continue working with clouds only.

⸻

16. Acceptance Criteria

The isobars layer is complete when:
	1.	it can be toggled independently
	2.	it is aligned to the same timeline as clouds
	3.	it does not require frontend time guessing
	4.	it remains readable above clouds and base map
	5.	it does not visually overload the map
	6.	missing isobar frames do not break playback
	7.	player behavior remains unchanged

⸻

17. Explicit Non-Goals

This enhancement does NOT introduce:
	•	pressure heatmaps
	•	pressure tendency fields
	•	frontal analysis
	•	cyclone classification
	•	frontend contour generation from raw grids
	•	local pressure scoring logic

⸻

18. Summary

This layer adds synoptic pressure context to the weather map.

It is:
	•	optional
	•	timeline-aligned
	•	weather-owned
	•	render-ready
	•	non-breaking to the current player/cloud system

